<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The entitlements the Fugaro console is signed with (desktop-console plan
	Task 4.1; design sections 5.1 rule 9 and 7.2, and section 12's item 4, which
	leaves this set unverified until a signed build has been run).

	The rule this file is written under is "the minimum": **no entitlement the
	app does not demonstrably use**. One key is granted, and it is granted for a
	reason that can be checked rather than assumed.

	com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit — granted. The hardened runtime denies a
	process the right to allocate memory that is both writable and executable,
	and V8 needs exactly that for its just-in-time compiler. Without the key the
	app is not merely slower: Electron aborts at startup on arm64. The console
	does not have its own JIT — this is V8's, inside the Electron framework it
	is built on.

	Granted this and deliberately **not**:

	com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — withheld. It is the key
	every Electron tutorial adds "just in case", and the only thing it buys is
	loading a library that is not signed by the same team. The console loads no
	plug-in and no native module of its own: its whole runtime surface is Node's
	own modules, the Electron framework and the processes of the CLI it spawns.
	The one dependency that could have forced it — electron-builder, which is a
	packer and not a runtime dependency — is not even installed here (see
	console/README.md). If a signed build ever fails without it, the failure is
	a fact this comment does not have, and the fix is an edit here with the
	codesign error quoted in the message.

	com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory — withheld. It is
	granted alongside disable-library-validation, for plug-ins that bring their
	own JIT, and the reason it is absent is the same one, one step further: we
	have a JIT (allow-jit above) and no plug-in to hand memory to.

	com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — withheld. It lets
	DYLD_* reach the process, which is the macOS twin of the NODE_OPTIONS hole
	the fuses close on the Node side (design section 5.1 rule 4): an environment
	variable that turns into code in our process.

	com.apple.security.cs.debugger and com.apple.security.get-task-allow —
	both withheld. Both let another process attach to a hardened-runtime app.
	get-task-allow is a development entitlement: a shipped build carrying it is
	debuggable by anything running as the user, whatever the binary's signature
	says. Nothing in this repository builds a debug variant that would need it.

	com.apple.security.app-sandbox — withheld. The console is not a Mac App
	Store build (design D4): it is signed with a Developer ID outside the store,
	and a sandbox key without a container and its file entitlements is a claim
	with nothing behind it.

	The device and network entitlements (com.apple.security.device.*,
	com.apple.security.network.client/server, the file-access keys) — all
	withheld, and they are withheld by design rather than by omission: the
	renderer reaches no network and no device, every permission request is denied
	by the main process rather than granted (design section 5.1 rule 9), and the
	only process in this application that talks to a network or reads a
	checkout's files is the CLI, which is a separate binary and brings its own
	signature.

	**Unverified, and named as such.** This repository has never produced a
	signed build: that is Check 59a, owner-run, on a Mac with a Developer ID
	certificate. Until it has run, the claim above is an argument from the
	bundle's contents — the app loads no plug-in, so it needs no plug-in key —
	rather than a measurement. Check 59a is where it becomes one, and a failure
	there is the signal to revisit this file rather than to widen it quietly.

	test/unit/entitlements.test.ts parses this file and asserts the set above
	exactly, with a named test per withheld key, so a widening here is a red test
	rather than a diff nobody reads.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
